WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.079
When you're choosing how an MCP client talks to a server,

2
00:00:03.079 --> 00:00:06.920
STDIO is one of two primary options.

3
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.159
It spawns the server as a subprocess

4
00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:12.760
and exchanges messages through stand-in and stand-out.

5
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.239
Let me walk you through how that works,

6
00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:17.079
when it's the right choice, and why

7
00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:20.600
Cloud Code uses STDIO natively.

8
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.280
STDIO transport works because of something

9
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.239
every process already has.

10
00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:27.840
When a program starts, the operating system

11
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.159
hands it standard streams, stand-in for input,

12
00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:34.520
stand-out for output, and stder for errors.

13
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.279
STDIO transport uses stand-in and stand-out

14
00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:41.959
as a bidirectional pipe for MCP messages,

15
00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:45.200
which is why it needs no network configuration at all.

16
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.319
The other half of the picture is the child process.

17
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:50.919
A child process is one that a parent process

18
00:00:50.919 --> 00:00:52.639
spawns and manages.

19
00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:55.540
And in this model, the MCP client is the parent,

20
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.639
and the server is the child.

21
00:00:57.639 --> 00:00:59.720
So the client launches the server,

22
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.439
keeps hold of its streams, and talks to it directly.

23
00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:06.760
That relationship is the whole transport.

24
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.599
Here's how STDIO transport actually

25
00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:11.680
works at the process level.

26
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.120
When the client starts, it reads its configuration

27
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.199
to find the command that launches

28
00:01:16.199 --> 00:01:18.639
each registered MCP server.

29
00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:21.660
It then spawns those servers as child processes,

30
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.879
using the operating system's standard process creation facilities.

31
00:01:25.879 --> 00:01:28.319
Once the child is running, the client and server

32
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:31.440
exchange new line delimited JSON messages.

33
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.800
Client writes to the child's stand-in.

34
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.959
Server reads from its own stand-in

35
00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:38.120
and writes responses to stand-out.

36
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.480
Client reads from the child's stand-out.

37
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.459
That's the entire communication model.

38
00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:46.099
There's no socket, no port, no HTTP layer.

39
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:48.739
The OS process table and pipe infrastructure

40
00:01:48.739 --> 00:01:50.040
handle everything.

41
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.080
This is fast, simple, and completely self-contained

42
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.319
on a single machine.

43
00:01:55.319 --> 00:01:59.239
The contrast between STDIO and network-based transports

44
00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:01.239
is sharper than it looks.

45
00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:05.879
STDIO uses OS-level pipes, not the network stack.

46
00:02:05.879 --> 00:02:09.720
There's no port to bind, no firewall rule to configure,

47
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.199
no TLS certificate to manage.

48
00:02:12.199 --> 00:02:15.160
It's inherently single user because the process

49
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.839
is private to the client that spawned it.

50
00:02:17.839 --> 00:02:20.279
Network transports do the opposite.

51
00:02:20.279 --> 00:02:23.600
The server binds to a port, listens for connections,

52
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.320
and handles authentication and encryption

53
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.279
to safely serve multiple clients.

54
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:33.000
Network transport is more complex to deploy and operate,

55
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.000
but it's the only option when the client and server

56
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.880
aren't on the same machine.

57
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.000
STDIO is simple precisely because it gives up everything

58
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.399
that network transport offers.

59
00:02:45.399 --> 00:02:48.759
STDIO fits best in three situations.

60
00:02:48.759 --> 00:02:50.559
Local development is the obvious one

61
00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:54.199
because running an MCP server as a subprocess on your dev

62
00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:58.800
machine is the fastest iteration loop while you build and test.

63
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.240
Desktop tool integrations are another,

64
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.800
since an application that bundles its own server

65
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.479
always has both components on the same machine.

66
00:03:06.479 --> 00:03:08.759
Then there is Cloud Code, which supports

67
00:03:08.759 --> 00:03:12.639
STDIO for local MCP servers and falls back

68
00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:16.240
to HTTP or SSE for remote ones.

69
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.240
You register a local server in Cloud Code settings

70
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.039
with the command that launches it,

71
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:25.520
and Cloud Code spawns it as a child process at session start.

72
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.639
What STDIO can't do matters just as much.

73
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:30.559
The moment a use case needs access

74
00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:33.679
from another host or a second simultaneous user,

75
00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:36.960
STDIO is the wrong transport.

76
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.279
Three characteristics define STDIO deployments.

77
00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:42.279
Zero configuration means there's nothing

78
00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:44.199
to set up on the network side.

79
00:03:44.199 --> 00:03:46.360
The OS handles the pipe.

80
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.199
Single user scope means one server process

81
00:03:49.199 --> 00:03:50.880
per client instance.

82
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.839
There's no concept of sharing.

83
00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:55.240
And subprocess lifecycle means the server

84
00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.839
starts when the client spawns it and exits

85
00:03:57.839 --> 00:03:59.500
when the client exits.

86
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.839
These three properties work together.

87
00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:04.360
Because each client owns its process,

88
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.199
and that process lives only for the session,

89
00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:10.639
there's no shared state between clients, no port conflicts,

90
00:04:10.639 --> 00:04:14.119
and no cleanup problem when a client disconnects.

91
00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:16.000
The simplicity is a feature when you're

92
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.920
operating in the right context.

93
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.679
But each of these properties is also a constraint.

94
00:04:21.679 --> 00:04:24.720
State management in STDIO is straightforward.

95
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.459
Because one server process is tied to one client session,

96
00:04:28.459 --> 00:04:32.000
the server can keep state in memory across all tool

97
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.279
calls within that session.

98
00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:36.320
You don't need to serialize state

99
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.079
to a database between calls because the process stays alive.

100
00:04:41.079 --> 00:04:43.239
A tool call made early in the session

101
00:04:43.239 --> 00:04:47.239
can produce a result that a later call reads from memory.

102
00:04:47.239 --> 00:04:50.040
When the client exits, the process exits,

103
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.760
and all in-memory state is released.

104
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.559
There's no risk of stale session state leaking to another user

105
00:04:56.559 --> 00:04:59.440
because each user gets their own process.

106
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.839
This model simplifies server implementation significantly.

107
00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:07.760
But it means STDIO can't serve a shared cache or shared resource

108
00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.600
across multiple clients.

109
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.119
STDIO has three limitations that

110
00:05:13.119 --> 00:05:15.040
are important for the exam.

111
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.480
No remote access.

112
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.679
Because the server is a subprocess,

113
00:05:18.679 --> 00:05:21.640
it's physically inaccessible from any other host.

114
00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.640
There's no address to connect to.

115
00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.440
No multi-user sharing.

116
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.720
Each user gets their own process.

117
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.959
Sharing a single STDIO server across clients

118
00:05:31.959 --> 00:05:33.920
isn't architecturally possible.

119
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.839
And STDIO isn't appropriate for production API deployments.

120
00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:40.279
If you're building a server that multiple users

121
00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:44.040
or multiple applications need to reach over a network,

122
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.839
STDIO isn't the right transport.

123
00:05:46.839 --> 00:05:47.920
These aren't bugs.

124
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.079
They're design choices that make STDIO simpler.

125
00:05:51.079 --> 00:05:56.760
But they do define a clear ceiling on where STDIO applies.

126
00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:02.799
A local MCP server under STDIO is really just another child process.

127
00:06:02.799 --> 00:06:05.399
The client owns its entire lifetime.

128
00:06:05.399 --> 00:06:07.640
There's no port to open and no session

129
00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.600
to resume because when your client goes away,

130
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.519
the server goes with it.

131
00:06:12.519 --> 00:06:15.640
Almost everything else about STDIO

132
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.040
falls out of that single relationship.

133
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.279
That's it for this one.

134
00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.600
I'll see you in the next lesson.

